{align:justify|Fishing is an exceedingly cruel pastime. Just imagine driving that sharp hook into your own lips or tongue — the pain would pierce to heart and lungs. No one could possibly bear it.}
{align:justify|Yet many people take fishing as a hobby and fish tirelessly. Some have even founded fishing clubs. The claim that "fishing trains the character" is nonsense — the talk of people without an ounce of compassion.}
{align:justify|Lam Giap Xuan was a Thai government official with a great passion for fishing and a member of the fishing club. On weekend holidays he would often take a boat out to sea to fish, sipping wine with tasty morsels while he waited — bliss beyond words. Everyone drank fine wine, ate fresh fish, and savored earthly pleasures to while away the hours.}
{align:justify|A few years back, when the club was newly founded, the atmosphere was lively and bustling. But since last year, membership has shriveled from 30 down to just 7 or 8.}
{align:justify|The club now hangs heavy with a deathly air; everyone is dispirited and no longer cares to go sea-fishing — and though Lam Giap Xuan is a stubborn man who does not believe in karmic retribution, a man with no family and no faith, even he cannot breathe life back into the club or rouse anyone to sail out fishing as before. The reason is two uncanny events, one after another.}
{align:justify|The first happened to old member A Ban himself. That Sunday, A Ban and his wife traveled inland to wish his mother-in-law a happy birthday. His mother-in-law was a person of standing and local prestige, and birthday guests packed the house. The feast was lavish — beef, pork, fish, chicken, duck, goose... whatever one could wish for. The guests feasted to their hearts' content and still could not finish it all.}
{align:justify|A Ban loved fish and innards; seeing a big fish on the banquet table, he was delighted. Eating carelessly, chewing poorly, and gulping down too many fish innards at once, he choked the moment he swallowed. Feeling something hard stuck in his throat, he frantically tried to pull it out with his fingers — but nothing could dislodge it. In that moment of being unable to swallow or pull it out, he could hardly breathe, and could not even open his mouth to cry for help.}
{align:justify|His younger brother, sitting opposite, was the first to notice the crisis and rushed over to help — but A Ban's eyes had already rolled back, his head slumped, and his breathing faded. Everyone rushed him to the emergency room, but halfway there he breathed his last.}
{align:justify|Though he was dead, the hospital still operated to determine the cause of death. The doctor pulled the fish innards lodged in his throat...}
{align:justify|And at that very instant, every doctor, nurse, and orderly present gasped in horror, eyes frozen and mouths stiff: the murderer was... a fish hook, lodged fast in A Ban's throat. Strangely, the hook had been hidden inside the fish's innards; once down A Ban's gullet it was squeezed outward, so the harder A Ban pulled, the tighter the hook gripped. Friends and relatives who witnessed this dreadful sight naturally thought at once of A Ban's beloved hobby of fishing — and his experience and skill had commanded the admiration of every club member. When others caught nothing, he hauled in plenty.}
{align:justify|Now, gazing at A Ban's rolled-back eyes and gaping mouth, beside the blood-covered hook, everyone's hair stood on end. No one could disbelieve that karmic retribution follows like shadow after form — what happened to A Ban was a thousand times, ten thousand times true!}
{align:justify|The second story is even stranger. Member Duong Ty Ich was a master angler who had entered tournaments and twice in a row taken the championship.}
{align:justify|Besides fishing, Mr. Duong loved riding his electric bicycle. Every day at dusk he would take his wife out to the suburbs for air.}
{align:justify|That night around 11 o'clock, after attending a close friend's funeral, he drove home. The road was so familiar he could have driven it blindfolded. But something most uncanny happened: while the car was running smoothly, he suddenly saw a great river appear before him — one he had never seen there before. To avoid plunging in, at 120 kilometers per hour he swerved hard and slammed the brakes. There was a tremendous CRASH — his car hit a power pole by the road. When he came to, he was lying in hospital.}
{align:justify|Everyone assumed Mr. Duong had crashed from speeding. That would have been an ordinary accident — not strange enough. What was strangest was that the wounds on his body healed quickly, except for his lips and palate, which were gravely injured. All his teeth had fallen out and he could eat nothing; for over a month food could only be pumped in liquid form through a tube into his throat. Stranger still were the tears above and below the corners of his mouth — doctors stitched them seven times and still they would not close.}
{align:justify|For every time they were stitched, when the sutures were removed the corners of his mouth festered and swelled again. Finally the doctors used the most advanced chemical sutures to sew them shut. A week later the stitches dissolved by themselves — but his lips were still ulcerated, both corners gaping wide like a fish's jaws. The attending physician had to admit defeat. For months he was racked with pain and endless misery, his mouth ulcerated and stretched wide, exactly like a fish's mouth torn and ulcerated by a hook.}
{align:justify|One day his wife came to visit as usual and blurted out without thinking: "Goodness! Your mouth gapes like that — just like a hooked fish's!"}
{align:justify|Those words struck Mr. Duong's conscience like a warning bell. He remembered how every time he caught a fish, he tore its mouth and ulcerated its whole palate. Only now did he truly feel its pain deep in his bones. So husband and wife hastened to the temple with incense and lamps; before the Buddha he bowed most sincerely in repentance and vowed never to fish again.}
{align:justify|Strange to tell, from that day on his lips gradually healed and were no longer ulcerated or swollen. Just a week later he was discharged. Afterward he told his fellow club members all he had been through and urged them to fish no more.}
{align:justify|From then on, a great many of the fishing club's members retired — a drastic decline. Before long Lam Xuan Giap too gave up fishing, and the club disbanded.}
